# A decay that cannot change the item no longer freezes the game loop. Game::startDecay() of an item with no time
# left calls Game::internalDecayItem(), which transforms the item to its decayTo and calls startDecay() again. When
# transformItem() returns the item unchanged (a decayTo of its own id, a decayTo of 0 or an unknown id in the item's
# own ITEM_ATTRIBUTE_DECAYTO, an item without an index in its parent) the two called each other forever; with -O3 the
# calls are compiled as jumps, so the dispatcher thread spun at 100% and the whole game stood still (no saves, no
# logins answered, every player frozen) until the process was killed.
# Found in the stress test (server/README.md "Stress test"): the base items.xml has item 23827 "dead dawnfly" with
# decayTo 23827 (the second stage of the Dawnfly corpse; the third stage 23828 is never reached). Every killed Dawnfly
# (52 spawns on the map, bestiary_spawns.lua) froze the server 15 minutes later, when its corpse reached that stage.
# Now: a decayTo of the item's own id or 0 removes the item (as decayTo 0 does; one console warning per item id), a
# negative one and a failed transform stop the decay (the item stays as it is). Every other decay is unchanged.
# Guard: tools/decay_loop_patch_test.py (CI). Applies after 0001-0032.
--- a/src/game.cpp
+++ b/src/game.cpp
@@ -5037,8 +5037,35 @@
